# A book's trust boundary is not its code. It is the set of files that decide
# what the book is allowed to say, so those are the ones that get a named
# reviewer.

*                           @konradcinkusz

# Every claim's provenance. A change here can promote an unverified source to
# strong, or delete an access date, and the page would carry the claim either
# way. This is the file the sourcing discipline rests on.
/sources.json               @konradcinkusz

# The one thing that keeps the kit company-independent. A term removed from the
# deny list is a term the build will accept forever after, silently.
/tools/denylist.txt         @konradcinkusz
/tools/check_generic.py     @konradcinkusz

# The gates, and the harness that proves they can fail. A weakened check and a
# passing build look identical from the outside.
/tools/check_sources.py     @konradcinkusz
/tools/test_gates.py        @konradcinkusz

# Appendix C is the kit's refusal list. Adding a figure here is easy and
# removing one should not be.
/chapters/app-refused.tex   @konradcinkusz

# Anything that runs with a token.
/.github/workflows/         @konradcinkusz
/.gitleaks.toml             @konradcinkusz
